{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What does a list comprehension do?",
      "options": [
        "It builds a new list from another one, in a single line",
        "It sorts a list in place",
        "It prints every element of a list",
        "It turns a list into a dictionary"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "In `[n * 2 for n in numbers]`, what is `n * 2`?",
      "options": [
        "The loop that goes through numbers",
        "The expression: what we do with each element",
        "The condition that filters the elements",
        "The name of the new list"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "What do the square brackets around a list comprehension do?",
      "options": [
        "They pick one element by its index",
        "They make the loop run twice",
        "They tell Python to put the results in a new list",
        "They change the original list"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "nums = [1, 2, 3]\ndoubled = [n * 2 for n in nums]\nprint(nums)",
      "options": ["[2, 4, 6]", "[1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 6]", "It stops with an error", "[1, 2, 3]"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "Which loop builds the same list as `squares = [n * n for n in nums]`?",
      "options": [
        "squares = []\nfor n in nums:\n    squares.append(n * n)",
        "squares = []\nfor n in nums:\n    squares.append(n)",
        "squares = []\nfor n in nums:\n    print(n * n)",
        "squares = []\nfor n in nums:\n    squares = n * n"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "What does the `if` at the end of `[n for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]` do?",
      "options": [
        "It stops the loop at the first even number",
        "It keeps only the elements for which the condition is True",
        "It changes every number into 0 or 1",
        "It runs the whole comprehension only if the list has even numbers"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "names = [\"Tommy Vercetti\", \"Lance\", \"Cortez\", \"Phil Cassidy\"]\nlong_names = [name for name in names if len(name) > 5]\nprint(long_names)",
      "options": [
        "['Tommy Vercetti', 'Phil Cassidy']",
        "['Lance']",
        "['Tommy Vercetti', 'Cortez', 'Phil Cassidy']",
        "[14, 6, 12]"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "names is `[\"tommy\", \"lance\"]`. Which line gives `['TOMMY', 'LANCE']`?",
      "options": [
        "[names.upper() for name in names]",
        "[name for name in names.upper()]",
        "[name.upper for name in names]",
        "[name.upper() for name in names]"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "In `[p * 0.9 for p in prices if p > 20]`, which prices get the 10% discount?",
      "options": [
        "Only those above 20: the if picks the elements first, then the expression changes them",
        "All of them: the if only decides the order",
        "Only those of 20 or less",
        "Only the first price above 20"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "When is a regular for loop a better choice than a list comprehension?",
      "options": [
        "Never: a comprehension can do everything a loop does, just as clearly",
        "When the logic gets complicated, with nested ifs or several lines of work",
        "When the list has more than 10 elements",
        "When the new list holds text, not numbers"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "scores is `[30, 42, 12]`. What does `[s for s in scores if s >= 50]` give?",
      "options": ["None", "[30, 42, 12]", "[], an empty list", "It stops with an error"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "In the Data Filter mission, readings is `[75, 30, 95, 88, 42, 92]`. What is in `high`?",
      "options": ["[75, 95, 88, 92]", "[150, 60, 190, 176, 84, 184]", "[30, 42]", "[95, 88, 92]"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "In the Data Filter mission, why is 75 in `passing` but not in `high`?",
      "options": [
        "passing keeps 50 or more and high keeps only above 80, and 75 is between them",
        "high skips the first reading",
        "passing is built before high",
        "75 is changed by doubled"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "In the Data Filter mission, how many elements does `doubled` have?",
      "options": [
        "3, only the high ones",
        "6, one for each reading: without an if, every element goes in",
        "4, only the passing ones",
        "12, twice as many as readings"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "In the Data Filter mission, which line builds `passing`?",
      "options": [
        "passing = [r for r in readings if r > 50]",
        "passing = [r >= 50 for r in readings]",
        "passing = [r for r in readings if r >= 50]",
        "passing = [r for r in readings if r > 80]"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "nums = [1, 5, 3, 7]\nchecks = [n > 3 for n in nums]\nprint(checks)",
      "options": ["[5, 7]", "[1, 3]", "2", "[False, True, False, True]"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "words = [\"sun\", \"moon\"]\nloud = [w + \"!\" for w in words]\nprint(loud)",
      "options": ["['sun!', 'moon!']", "['sun', 'moon', '!']", "sun!moon!", "['!sun', '!moon']"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q18",
      "text": "What is wrong with `doubled = [n * 2 for x in nums]`?",
      "options": [
        "Nothing, Python finds n by itself",
        "n isn't defined: the variable after for is x, so the expression must use x",
        "The brackets must be round",
        "nums must come before for"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q19",
      "text": "Which of these is a list comprehension?",
      "options": [
        "for n in nums: n * 2",
        "[for n in nums: n * 2]",
        "[n * 2 for n in nums]",
        "n * 2 for n in nums"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q20",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "nums = [4, 9, 2]\nsmall = [n for n in nums if n < 5]\nprint(small[0])",
      "options": ["[4]", "9", "2", "4"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "names = [\"ana\", \"rex\"]\nnice = [n[0].upper() + n[1:] for n in names]\nprint(nice)",
      "options": ["['Ana', 'Rex']", "['ANA', 'REX']", "['A', 'R']", "['ana', 'rex']"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ndoubled = [n * 2 for n in nums]\nbig = [d for d in doubled if d > 4]\nprint(big)",
      "options": ["[3, 4]", "[6, 8]", "[2, 4, 6, 8]", "[8]"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "Which list comprehension builds the same list as this loop?",
      "code": "evens = []\nfor n in nums:\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        evens.append(n)",
      "options": [
        "[n % 2 == 0 for n in nums]",
        "[n for n in nums if n % 2 == 1]",
        "[n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0]",
        "[n * 2 for n in nums]"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q24",
      "text": "Where does the `if` go in a list comprehension that filters?",
      "options": [
        "At the start, before the expression",
        "Outside the brackets",
        "Between for and in",
        "At the end, after the for ... in ... part"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q25",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "prices = [3, 8, 12]\nlabels = [f\"{p} EUR\" for p in prices]\nprint(labels)",
      "options": [
        "['3 EUR', '8 EUR', '12 EUR']",
        "['{p} EUR', '{p} EUR', '{p} EUR']",
        "3 EUR\n8 EUR\n12 EUR",
        "['3', '8', '12', 'EUR']"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q26",
      "text": "A list comprehension without an if goes through a list of 8 elements. How many elements does the new list have?",
      "options": ["1", "8", "0", "16"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q27",
      "text": "A list comprehension with an if goes through a list of 8 elements. How many elements can the new list have?",
      "options": [
        "Always 8",
        "Always fewer than 8",
        "Any number from 0 to 8, depending on how many pass the condition",
        "Always at least 1"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q28",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "names = [\"tommy\", \"lance\"]\nloud = [names.upper() for name in names]\nprint(loud)",
      "options": [
        "It prints ['TOMMY', 'LANCE']",
        "It prints ['TOMMY LANCE']",
        "It prints []",
        "It stops with an error: names is a list, and a list has no upper()"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q29",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "scores = [85, 42, 91]\npassed = [s for s in scores if s >= 50]\nscores.append(60)\nprint(passed)",
